  },
  {
    q: "Why Does My 1969 Fender Have 1966 Pots?",
    a: "Because that's how CBS bought parts. Fender took delivery of an enormous lot of CTS pots in the mid-1960s and kept pulling from that supply for about five years. Pots three or four years older than the guitar are the normal, correct result on a CBS-era Fender, not evidence of a swap. It's the most common reason a perfectly original guitar gets wrongly called a parts guitar.",
  },
  {
    q: "My Pot Has Two Different Numbers On It. Which One Is The Date?",
    a: "The date code is the group that starts with a maker code, 134, 137, 140, or 304. The other number is usually a Fender factory part number such as 021357. The fastest way to tell is the last two digits: a week only runs from 01 to 52, so if those digits come out higher than 52, you aren't looking at a date.",
  },
  {
    q: "What If My Fender Has Two Different Pot Dates?",
    a: "That's common and fine when the dates sit within a few weeks or months of each other. Majority rules. If three pots read early 1962 and one reads late 1963, the odd one out is almost certainly a replacement, not the build date.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can A Pot Code Fake A Vintage Date?",
    a: "Not without swapping the whole pot, and a replaced pot gives itself away with fresh solder and a date that breaks from the others. That difficulty is exactly why original pots are one of the most reliable dating tools you have.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does A Correct Pot Code Prove My Guitar Is All Original?",
    a: "No. It dates that one pot. Originality is a whole-guitar judgment that weighs the neck date, the pickups, the tuners, the hardware, and the finish together. Send me three photos and I'll give you the real story for free.",
  },
];

